---
import Work from "@layouts/Work.astro";

export const frontmatter = {
    order: 5,
    title: "Rainbow School",
    coverImage: "/rainbowschool/Axon NW.jpg",
};
---

<Work
    title={frontmatter.title}
    subtitle="A Journey Through Light, Instinct, and Play"
    description="Rooted in the belief that learning emerges through play, this kindergarten invites children to engage with space through curiosity, challenge, and subconscious exploration. The design began with two conceptual seeds: a blindfolded drawing exercise and the folklore of Red Riding Hood. The resulting drawing revealed a woven rope-like section, inspiring a massing strategy of overlapping cubes that form spatial volumes. The landscape echoes this logic, composed of square platforms and connective slits that encourage movement and discovery."
    coverImage={frontmatter.coverImage}
    coverImageAlt={frontmatter.title}
    course="Architectural Studio II"
    courseDescription="Learning by Playing or A Kindergartener's Approach to Architecture"
    advisor="Daniel Horowitz"
    collaborators=""
    date="Spring 2020"
    location=""
    gallery={import.meta.glob("/public/rainbowschool/gallery/*")}
>
    <section class="stack">
        <p>
            Rooted in the belief that learning emerges through play, this
            kindergarten invites children to engage with space through
            curiosity, challenge, and subconscious exploration. The design began
            with two conceptual seeds: a blindfolded drawing exercise and the
            folklore of Red Riding Hood. The drawing, made without visual
            control, produced a dense, woven field of lines. A single square was
            extracted from this field for close analysis, becoming the geometric
            seed for a massing strategy of overlapping cubes. The landscape
            echoes this logic, composed of interlocking square platforms and
            connective slits that encourage movement and discovery.
        </p>
        <p>
            In Red Riding Hood, as in most stories, the protagonist's journey is
            shaped by the trials she encounters along the way. The school adopts
            this narrative structure as a spatial experience. Children navigate
            the building through climbing, crawling, and walking, fostering both
            passive and active knowledge through embodied interaction. The
            architecture challenges and supports, offering a playground of
            layered cognition where instinct precedes logic.
        </p>
        <p>
            Throughout the day, colored glazing filters sunlight into shifting
            patterns of light and shadow. Polygonal windows cast vibrant shapes
            across floors and walls, creating an ever-changing environment that
            quietly reinforces lessons in color, form, and perception. The name
            Rainbow School reflects this choreography of light — a spatial
            language that speaks to children before they learn to name it.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Blindfolded Drawing Study</h3>
        <p>
            A blindfolded drawing exercise produced a dense field of intertwined
            lines, created through feel, speed, and pressure rather than sight.
            When the drawing was examined, its woven quality suggested a logic
            of interlacing and overlap. A single square was extracted from the
            field for close study, becoming the basis for two parallel
            explorations: a 2D system of interlocking squares and a 3D system of
            overlapping cubes.
        </p>
        <p>
            In the 2D study, the square underwent a series of operations —
            cutouts that formed slits, extrusion to introduce thickness,
            directional arrangement, and interlocking — to generate a volumetric
            system. In the 3D study, cubes were overlapped to create spatial
            density, then opened by rotating a final cube and using it as a
            subtractive cut. Together, these operations transformed an
            instinctive drawing into a spatial framework, establishing the
            massing logic and circulation conditions of the building.
        </p>

        <div class="grid auto-fit">
            <img
                src="/rainbowschool/Drawing (Cut).jpg"
                alt="Blind-folded Drawing"
            />
            <img
                src="/rainbowschool/Drawing (Annotated).png"
                alt="Analysis of Blind-folded Drawing"
            />
            <img src="/rainbowschool/2D Study.jpg" alt="2D Study" />
            <img src="/rainbowschool/3D Study.jpg" alt="3D Study" />
        </div>
        <img src="/rainbowschool/Squares Diagram.jpg" alt="Squares Diagram" />
        <img src="/rainbowschool/Boxes Diagram.jpg" alt="Boxes Diagram" />
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Folktale Study: Red Riding Hood</h3>
        <p>
            The folktale was analyzed through six behavioral stages that shape
            the protagonist's journey: preference, instruction, temptation,
            perception, reaction, and reflection. These stages reveal how
            children naturally move through the world and how architecture can
            support or amplify those tendencies.
        </p>

        <img
            class="diagram"
            src="/rainbowschool/Red Riding Hood.png"
            alt="Program & Section"
            style="width:auto; height:240px; object-fit: scale-down;"
        />

        <table class="rounded-corners">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th class="borderless"></th>
                    <th class="rounded-top-left">Red Riding Hood</th>
                    <th class="rounded-top-right">Lesson</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <th class="rounded-top-left">Preference</th>
                    <td>
                        Girl is gifted a red cape and instinctively adopts it as
                        part of her identity.
                    </td>
                    <td>Instinctive Attachment</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Instruction</th>
                    <td>
                        Mother sends Girl to Grandma's house and tells her to
                        stay on the path.
                    </td>
                    <td>Guided Boundaries</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Temptation</th>
                    <td>
                        Wolf convinces Girl to leave the path and pick flowers
                        as a gift for Grandma.
                    </td>
                    <td>Curiosity Detours</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Perception</th>
                    <td>
                        Girld notices and questions the strange features in the
                        disguised Wolf.
                    </td>
                    <td>Awareness Through Noticing</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Reaction</th>
                    <td>
                        Wolf reveals his identiy and pounces at Girl. Girl
                        reacts quickly and escapes.
                    </td>
                    <td>Movement as Cognition</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th>Reflection</th>
                    <td>
                        Girl frees Grandma and recounts the events,
                        understanding the lesson through experience.
                    </td>
                    <td>Understanding Through Pause</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
            <tfoot>
                <tr>
                    <th class="rounded-bottom-left">Summary</th>
                    <td>
                        The real lesson is not the destination but the trials
                        along the way.
                    </td>
                    <td class="rounded-bottom-right">Process as Curriculum</td>
                </tr>
            </tfoot>
        </table>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <h3>Concept</h3>

        <div class="grid auto-fit">
            <div class="stack">
                <h4 class="center">Shapes</h4>
                <img src="/rainbowschool/Shapes.webp" alt="Shapes" />
                <p>
                    The building's geometry follows the system developed in the
                    3D study. Overlapping cubes create a varied set of walls,
                    floors, and ceilings, making each surface distinct in shape
                    and proportion. During the study, the model was
                    paper-mâchéed with motifs of <a
                        href="https://www.artchive.com/artwork/three-figures-fernand-leger-1921/"
                        >Fernand Leger's
                        <i>Three figures</i>
                    </a>. The shapes and compositions in that artwork informed
                    the placement and geometry of the windows. This results in a
                    building where geometric variety is not decorative but
                    structural — a spatial language shaped directly by the
                    design process.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="stack">
                <h4 class="center">Colors</h4>
                <img src="/rainbowschool/Colors.jpg" alt="Colors" />
                <p>
                    The color strategy also draws from Three Figures. Its
                    palette guided the glazing colors applied to the windows,
                    allowing daylight to carry those hues into the interior. As
                    sunlight shifts, these colors animate surfaces and create
                    subtle gradients, giving children a quiet, intuitive way to
                    experience color and light. The glazing becomes a soft
                    perceptual cue, reinforcing preference, curiosity, and
                    spatial association.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="stack">
                <h4 class="center">Movement</h4>
                <img src="/rainbowschool/Movement.jpg" alt="Movement" />
                <p>
                    Movement is calibrated to the scale and abilities of
                    kindergarteners. Ramps and stairs dominate the lower levels,
                    offering accessible routes that build confidence through
                    repetition. Higher levels introduce ladders and more
                    challenging transitions. For young children, these elements
                    function as climbing tasks that encourage persistence and
                    physical problem-solving. As children grow more confident,
                    they naturally progress toward routes with greater
                    difficulty. The building's circulation is therefore
                    structured as a gradient of challenge, supporting
                    exploration and embodied learning.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <img
            src="/rainbowschool/Program & Section.jpg"
            alt="Program & Section"
        />
    </section>
</Work>
